1️⃣ Purpose of Ontology Quality Governance

To ensure:
✔ Consistency — Standardized class, property, and instance naming
✔ Interoperability — Alignment with upper-level ontologies (BFO, CCO, IOF)
✔ Maintainability — Clear patterns facilitate collaborative development
✔ Traceability — Everything is verifiable, linked to sources, SMEs, and change logs
✔ Machine Reasoning Readiness — Optimized for logical inference, SPARQL queries, reasoning engines

2️⃣ Layers of Quality Governance
Governance Layer	Description
Conceptual Quality	Is the class logically correct? Does it represent a single concept?
Structural Quality	Is it correctly placed in class hierarchy? Domain/range?
Linguistic Quality	Does the label follow naming conventions (e.g., PascalCase)?
Semantic Quality	Is the concept aligned with BFO/CCO/IOF/Industrial standards?
Technical Quality	Validation in Protégé, Stardog, OWL reasoners, SHACL rules
Change/Version Quality	Has it passed review, change control, SME approval?

3️⃣ Naming Convention Principles

⚖ Goal: Human-readable, machine-interpretable, logically precise.

🏷 Class Naming
Rule	Example	Notes
PascalCase	AircraftEngine, CompressorBlade	No spaces, no punctuation
Singular nouns	InspectionReport, not InspectionReports	Represents a class, not individuals
Avoid abbreviations unless standard	LCFFailureMode, but prefer LowCycleFatigueFailureMode	Abbrev. allowed only if globally accepted
Semantic alignment	Align with BFO, CCO categories — InformationContentEntity, Process, MaterialComponent	Reflect ontological meaning


👤 Individual Naming
Rule	Example
Use snake_case or concatenated	engine_SN12345, inspection_2024_10_05
Include identifiers	Serial number, Part number, Work Order ID
Use underscores, not spaces	engine_maintenance_2025



